{:edb/db-before-t 827 :edb/db-after-t 828 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534140 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:09:51.969Z" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119665 1071 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](images/38/3813c34b623ef9ad6f7ca534fb3a6e373d4ce1961dab49dfab9e414fea84daae.svg)" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119665 1071 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](images/fd/fd6c1665506e5186c1ccac2cf669040943bbc31347b27fae1c77ffe9b01ed542.png)" 13194139534140 17592186045417 false] [17592186119775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411964 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411965 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411967 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411968 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411969 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411970 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411971 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411966 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411963 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186119994 1050 420.0 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186120455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118844 13194139534140 17592186045417 false] [17592186120456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118850 13194139534140 17592186045417 false] [17592186121020 1064 "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations:\n\nI. Swapping two rows\n\nII. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\nIII. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix $A = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Taking the determinant, we have $$\\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1 \\cdot 4 - 2 \\cdot 3 = -2.$$\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =2 \\\\ & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =-4 \\\\ & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\ & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534140 17592186045417 false] [17592186121020 1064 "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations: \n\n  I. Swapping two rows\n\n  II. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\n  III. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix ![](images/fa/fa51d7c585db408c47e8640ae839c1a3bbf94e2b0097e1ade511e5773461f1e8.svg) Taking the determinant, we have\n![](images/bc/bcf5c5dc9f5b7ed3849e719665f802040cf51e3334ae99e25ea7648cbb0f6134.svg)\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186121045 1064 "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n![](images/08/0826c05fe2eabec96065eb2c83228168129b42c309764df4b101f4a520c4a0f9.svg)\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=k\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186121045 1064 "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have $$\\left| \\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=k\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534140 17592186045417 false] [17592186121061 1064 "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n![](images/fe/fe02091dde43a8a2405f6b588f619bc9fa3f54b31ac8a1ac39d318761f00cc3e.svg)\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186121061 1064 "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\\left| \\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = {\\color{red}3}^2 \\cdot \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}$$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." 13194139534140 17592186045417 false] [17592186411909 1080 "-2" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411909 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411909 1083 #uuid "01085bc4-2d6b-556c-908d-a086f143e25f" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411910 1080 "-51" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411910 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411910 1083 #uuid "116428ff-2d2f-5e28-a226-5dbcdd1ff147" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411911 1080 "60" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411911 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411911 1083 #uuid "132747fd-8145-5600-a67f-e5fedabc2538" 13194139534140 17592186045417 true] [17592186411912 